src/backend/access/heap/README.HOT-INDEXED

HOT-indexed updates (Selective Index Update, "SIU")
====================================================

Classic HOT (see README.HOT) keeps an UPDATE off the indexes only when the
update changes NO indexed column: the new tuple becomes a heap-only member of
the same t_ctid chain, every index entry keeps pointing at the chain root, and
because all live versions share the same indexed values a reader that walks the
chain always lands on a tuple whose values match the index entry it arrived
through.

HOT-indexed relaxes that to "the update may change indexed columns and still
stay on the same page", by inserting a fresh index entry only into the indexes
whose attributes actually changed, and leaving a per-hop record on the page of
which indexed attributes changed.  The old index entries are now potentially
STALE -- an entry for an old key still chain-leads to the live tuple, whose
current key is different -- so readers must be taught to recognise and discard
a stale arrival.

This file documents the on-disk encoding, the eligibility rules, the write and
read paths, chain pruning/vacuum, crash recovery, and the limitations.  It uses
the following notation throughout:

    LP[n]                 line pointer at offset n on the page
    vN                    the N'th heap tuple version in a chain
    INDEXED_UPDATED{a,b}  tuple carries HEAP_INDEXED_UPDATED; this hop changed
                          indexed attributes a and b
    idx_a: (a=11) -> LP[2] an idx_a leaf with key a=11 pointing at LP[2]
    1. ... -> ...         a numbered step in a chain walk; the trailing "keep"
                          or "drop" marks the read-side staleness outcome


The classic-HOT invariant we are relaxing
------------------------------------------

Classic HOT relies on: every live btree leaf resolves, via a same-page t_ctid
walk, to a live heap tuple whose indexed values equal the leaf's key.

HOT-indexed breaks the "values equal the leaf's key" half: a chain may contain
tuples with different indexed values, and a leaf for an old value still reaches
the live tuple.  Two mechanisms restore correctness:

  - a per-hop on-page record (the "tombstone") naming which indexed attributes
    changed, and
  - a read-side test that, for the index a scan arrived through, asks whether
    any hop crossed between the arriving leaf's target and the live tuple
    changed an attribute that index covers.  If so the leaf is stale and is
    discarded; if not the leaf still matches and the tuple is kept.  The test
    is a bitmap overlap (no key comparison and no index tuple required).


The tombstone line pointer
--------------------------

A tombstone is an LP_NORMAL line pointer whose HeapTupleHeader is NOT a real
tuple.  It is distinguished from a real tuple by the pair

    (t_infomask2 & HEAP_INDEXED_UPDATED) != 0   AND   HeapTupleHeaderGetNatts == 0

No real heap tuple ever sets HEAP_INDEXED_UPDATED, so the pair is a signature no
genuine tuple can produce.  (natts == 0 alone is not exclusive to tombstones --
a zero-column relation stores rows with natts == 0 -- but such relations have no
indexed attributes and never carry HEAP_INDEXED_UPDATED, so the conjunction is
unambiguous.)  Tombstones set HEAP_XMIN_INVALID | HEAP_XMAX_INVALID, so every
visibility routine treats them as not-a-tuple and skips them.

There are two variants:

    Adjacent tombstone   written by heap_update next to a new HOT-indexed tuple.
                         t_ctid = (InvalidBlockNumber, target_offnum), where
                         target_offnum is the live HOT-indexed tuple it
                         describes.  Body: uint16 t_nbytes + uint8 bitmap[],
                         the per-update modified-indexed-attrs bitmap.

    Bridge tombstone     written by pruneheap in place of a dead mid-chain
                         HOT-indexed heap-only tuple whose LP cannot yet be
                         reclaimed (stale leaves still point at it).
                         t_ctid = (current_blkno, forward_offnum) -- a same-page
                         forward chain link.  No bitmap.  Fixed size
                         HOT_INDEXED_BRIDGE_SIZE = MAXALIGN(SizeofHeapTupleHeader).

The target/forward offset is read from t_ctid.offnum
(HotIndexedTombstoneGetTarget); it is not duplicated in the body.
HeapTupleHeaderIsHotIndexedTombstone matches both variants;
HeapTupleHeaderIsHotIndexedBridge (t_ctid.blockno is valid) tells them apart.

See access/hot_indexed.h for the accessors and the StaticAssertDecls that pin
the layout.


Eligibility: HeapUpdateHotAllowable
-----------------------------------

The executor computes modified_idx_attrs (the indexed attributes this UPDATE
changed; INDEX_ATTR_BITMAP_INDEXED, which includes key, INCLUDE, expression and
partial-predicate columns).  HeapUpdateHotAllowable() returns one of:

    HEAP_HOT_MODE_NO        non-HOT update: new tuple on a fresh TID, every
                            index gets a fresh entry (the pre-HOT-indexed path).
    HEAP_HOT_MODE_CLASSIC   classic HOT: no index changes at all.
    HEAP_HOT_MODE_INDEXED   HOT-indexed: heap-only new tuple + adjacent
                            tombstone; only changed-attr indexes get a fresh
                            entry.

Decision order:

    1. modified_idx_attrs empty                         -> CLASSIC
    2. all modified attrs belong only to summarizing
       indexes (BRIN, ...)                              -> CLASSIC
    3. relation is a system catalog                     -> NO
    4. relation has an exclusion constraint             -> NO
    5. UPDATE touches more than hot_indexed_update_
       threshold percent of indexed attrs (default 80)  -> NO
    6. otherwise                                        -> INDEXED
       (heap_update further caps chain length, below)

Steps 1 and 2 are reached before step 3, so system catalogs keep classic HOT
unchanged; only catalog updates that would otherwise be HOT-indexed fall back to
a non-HOT update.  See "System catalogs" below.

Non-btree and expression indexes need no exemption: the read-side test is a
bitmap overlap on the index's referenced heap attributes
(RelationGetIndexedAttrs), which is defined for every access method and needs
no key evaluation, so a stale leaf is recognised for any index kind.


The write path
--------------

For HEAP_HOT_MODE_INDEXED, heap_update:

  1. checks the page has room for BOTH the new tuple and an adjacent tombstone;
     if not, it downgrades to HEAP_HOT_MODE_NO (off-page, fully indexed);
  2. writes the new heap-only tuple, t_ctid-linked into the chain, with
     HEAP_INDEXED_UPDATED set;
  3. writes an adjacent tombstone (heap_build_hot_indexed_tombstone) carrying
     the modified-attrs bitmap for this hop;
  4. emits an UPDATE WAL record flagged XLH_UPDATE_CONTAINS_TOMBSTONE that
     carries the tombstone bytes so replay reconstructs both items.

Which indexes get a fresh entry is decided by ExecSetIndexUnchanged(): for each
index it consults RelationGetIndexedAttrs() (the per-index attribute set,
including INCLUDE columns) and sets ii_IndexUnchanged when that set does not
overlap modified_idx_attrs.  Unchanged non-summarizing indexes are skipped;
changed indexes (and all summarizing indexes) receive a fresh entry whose TID
points directly at the new heap-only tuple.


The chain and the two kinds of leaf entry
------------------------------------------

After a HOT-indexed update there are two kinds of btree leaf for an indexed
column:

    Fresh entry   inserted by the update; its key is the new value and its TID
                  points directly at the heap-only tuple that was current at
                  insert time.

    Stale entry   the pre-update entry; its key is an old value and its TID
                  still points at the chain root (or an earlier member).

A chain walk that crosses a tuple carrying HEAP_INDEXED_UPDATED, or that lands
directly on a live HOT-indexed heap-only tuple, is a "hot-indexed hop".  The
read side decides staleness from the modified-attrs bitmaps of the hops crossed
strictly after the arriving leaf's target.


Read-side correctness: bitmap overlap
-------------------------------------

heap_hot_search_buffer walks the chain, transparently following bridge
tombstones (HotIndexedBridgeGetForward) and stopping at adjacent tombstones.
For each chain member it advances to *after* the entry tuple it records the
member's offset; on reaching the visible tuple it tests, for each recorded
member, that member's modified-attrs bitmap (read from its retained adjacent
tombstone) against index_attrs -- the set of heap attributes the arriving index
covers (RelationGetIndexedAttrs, cached by index_fetch_heap and passed down):

    overlap empty     -> none of this index's attributes changed between the
                         leaf's target and the live tuple; the leaf still
                         matches.  Keep the tuple.
    overlap non-empty -> an attribute the index covers changed on the way; the
                         leaf is stale.  Drop it.  The fresh entry for the live
                         tuple's current value returns the row through its own
                         path.

The entry tuple's own producing hop is excluded (a fresh entry pointing directly
at the tuple it was inserted for is never dropped).  The result is surfaced as
IndexScanDesc.xs_hot_indexed_stale; IndexScan and IndexOnlyScan drop the tuple
when it is set.  BitmapHeapScan cannot identify the originating index, so for it
index_attrs is NULL and the flag instead means "a hot-indexed hop was crossed",
which forces the bitmap scan's recheck qual.

This "fresh entry re-supplies the row" property is why dropping a stale leaf
never loses a row: the live tuple's current value always has a fresh entry, and
a scan for that value finds it.

Worked example.  User table siu_demo(id, a, b, c) with btree indexes idx_a,
idx_b, idx_c, fillfactor leaving page room.  Three single-column updates:

    UPDATE a=11;  UPDATE b=21;  UPDATE c=31;

  Chain on one page (t_ctid: LP[1]->LP[3]->LP[5]->LP[7]; adjacent tombstones at
  the even LPs, each carrying its hop's modified-attrs bitmap):
    LP[1]: v1  a=10 b=20 c=30   root, dead
    LP[3]: v2  a=11 b=20 c=30   heap-only, dead,  INDEXED_UPDATED{a}
    LP[5]: v3  a=11 b=21 c=30   heap-only, dead,  INDEXED_UPDATED{b}
    LP[7]: v4  a=11 b=21 c=31   heap-only, LIVE,  INDEXED_UPDATED{c}

  Index entries (fresh point at the version current when the value was set):
    idx_a:  (a=10) -> LP[1]  stale     (a=11) -> LP[3]  fresh
    idx_b:  (b=20) -> LP[1]  stale     (b=21) -> LP[5]  fresh
    idx_c:  (c=30) -> LP[1]  stale     (c=31) -> LP[7]  fresh

  Query 1 -- current value via fresh entry:  WHERE a = 11   (idx_a -> LP[3])
    1. enter LP[3] (v2); hops crossed after it: {b} at LP[5], {c} at LP[7]
    2. walk LP[3] -> LP[5] -> LP[7] LIVE, return v4
    3. overlap(idx_a {a}, {b,c}) = empty -> keep
  Query 2 -- obsolete value via stale entry:  WHERE a = 10   (idx_a -> LP[1])
    1. enter LP[1] (v1, dead root); hops crossed after it: {a},{b},{c}
    2. walk LP[1] -> ... -> LP[7] LIVE, return v4
    3. overlap(idx_a {a}, {a,b,c}) = {a} -> drop
       (a=10 no longer exists; correctly returns no row through this entry)
  Query 3 -- current value whose fresh entry is direct:  WHERE c = 31
    1. enter LP[7] (v4, live); it is the entry tuple, its own {c} hop excluded
    2. no hops crossed after it
    3. overlap(idx_c {c}, {}) = empty -> keep

The same test serves equality, range and inequality IndexScans: the leaf was
returned by the index only because its key satisfied the scan, and overlap-empty
means that key still describes the live tuple, so keeping it is correct for any
scan shape.

The test needs only the index's referenced heap attributes, available for every
access method, so all non-summarizing indexes -- including non-btree AMs and
btree indexes with expression keys -- participate.  Expression, predicate and
INCLUDE columns are covered because RelationGetIndexedAttrs reports the heap
attributes they reference.

Unique checks.  _bt_check_unique fetches the conflicting tuple under
SnapshotDirty, passing the unique index's attribute set, and applies the same
overlap test.  A non-empty overlap means the candidate leaf is stale for this
index and is skipped (not a conflict); this also subsumes an index key cycled
X -> Y -> X, since the X attribute changed along the path and so overlaps.  Such
skipped-but-live arrivals set prevalldead = false so the still-live-resolving
leaf is never marked LP_DEAD.


The read side needs no index tuple
----------------------------------

The bitmap-overlap test reads only the per-hop tombstones on the heap page and
the arriving index's attribute set; it never inspects the stored leaf key.  So
plain index scans do not request xs_want_itup for HOT-indexed purposes, and no
"this relation may contain tombstones" predicate is needed: a scan that crosses
no hot-indexed hop computes an empty overlap and keeps every tuple, exactly as
before the feature.  (IndexOnlyScan still sets xs_want_itup for its own reason
-- it serves values out of the index tuple.)


Bridge tombstones (mid-chain preservation)
------------------------------------------

When pruneheap finds a dead mid-chain HOT-indexed heap-only tuple it cannot
simply reclaim the LP to LP_UNUSED: a stale btree leaf may still point at it,
and a reused LP would make that leaf resolve to an unrelated tuple.  Instead it
rewrites the LP in place as a bridge tombstone.  To keep the read-side
bitmap-overlap test correct, bridges are linked in chain order rather than all
jumping to the first live member: each bridge forwards to the NEXT surviving
member (the last to first_live), and the bridged tuple's own adjacent tombstone
is retained, keyed by the bridge's offset.  A reader arriving through any stale
leaf thus traverses every surviving hop in turn and picks up each hop's
modified-attrs bitmap, so the overlap it computes is exactly the set of
attributes changed between its leaf's target and the live tuple.  When the
collapsed prefix contains a HOT-indexed hop the chain root is collapsed to a
HOT-indexed data redirect (an LP_REDIRECT carrying a bitmap, see below) that
points straight at first_live; a purely classic dead prefix uses a plain
LP_REDIRECT to first_live.  The next index-cleanup pass of VACUUM removes the
stale leaves, after which the bridges (and their retained tombstones) are
reclaimed.

HOT-indexed data redirect.  A plain LP_REDIRECT carries no bitmap, so a leaf
pointing at the root cannot be tested for staleness without traversing the
chain -- and the chain members it would traverse are exactly the ones VACUUM
wants to reclaim.  The chain root is therefore collapsed to a *data* redirect:
an LP_REDIRECT with lp_len > 0 whose body (HotIndexedRedirectData, written over
the dead root tuple's freed bytes) holds the redirect target plus the union of
the modified-attrs bitmaps of every hop from the root to first_live.  The
target is always first_live (a live tuple, never a reclaimable bridge), so the
redirect never dangles when an intermediate bridge is later reclaimed.  A
root-pointing leaf reads the union directly (heap_hot_indexed_redirect_overlaps)
and is dropped if it overlaps the scanning index -- no traversal needed.  The
union is computed at prune time by heap_page_prune_execute, which walks the
on-page chain (still intact -- the now-unused reclaim runs afterwards) OR-ing
each crossed member's adjacent-tombstone bitmap.  A single-hop chain (a dead
root over its immediate live successor) has no mid-chain leaves, so the lone
successor tombstone is reclaimed -- its bitmap now lives in the redirect.  A
multi-hop chain keeps every tombstone (mid-chain leaves still consult them) and
the union duplicates them.  A re-collapse (the root is already a data redirect
whose target has since died) re-points the redirect at the new first_live,
seeding the union from the existing blob and extending it across the new hops.

Collapse transition.  Take the worked example's chain after v1, v2 and v3 have
died (only v4 is live).  Before pruning the page holds the tuples and their
adjacent tombstones (t_ctid forward links shown with ->):

    LP[1]: v1  root, dead                -> LP[3]
    LP[3]: v2  dead, INDEXED_UPDATED{a}  -> LP[5]
    LP[5]: v3  dead, INDEXED_UPDATED{b}  -> LP[7]
    LP[7]: v4  LIVE, INDEXED_UPDATED{c}
    LP[2],LP[4],LP[6]: tombstones for {a},{b},{c} (target LP[3],LP[5],LP[7])

After the collapse the root becomes a data redirect to first_live carrying the
union {a,b,c}; each dead mid-chain member becomes a bridge to the next
survivor, and the mid-chain tombstones are kept:

    LP[1]: data-redirect{a,b,c} -> LP[7]   (was v1 root)
    LP[3]: bridge -> LP[5]    (was v2)
    LP[5]: bridge -> LP[7]    (was v3)
    LP[7]: v4  LIVE, INDEXED_UPDATED{c}
    LP[4],LP[6]: tombstones {b},{c}, retained for mid-chain leaves

A stale idx_a: (a=10) -> LP[1] reads the redirect union {a,b,c} directly; the
overlap with idx_a's {a} is non-empty, so the stale leaf is dropped.  A
mid-chain stale leaf idx_b: -> LP[3] still walks LP[3] -> LP[5] -> LP[7],
crossing tombstones {b},{c}.

Chain-root lookups during pruning use a per-page predecessor map built once
(heap_prune_build_pred_map / PruneState.pred[]); the page is not modified during
the planning phase, so a single O(maxoff) build serves every lookup and the walk
itself is O(chain length).

Chain-match relaxation.  Classic HOT requires prev_xmax of step N to equal xmin
of step N+1.  Bridges carry HEAP_XMIN_INVALID | HEAP_XMAX_INVALID, so
heap_hot_search_buffer does not advance prev_xmax across a bridge hop; the chain
check resumes at the bridge's forward target.  Bridges deliberately relax the
strict chain-match on the hop they replace; the bitmap-overlap test on the
reader side absorbs the relaxation.

Crash recovery.  Bridges are produced by the prune WAL record
(XLHP_HAS_HOT_INDEXED_BRIDGES) and reproduced byte-for-byte by replay, which
also sets PD_HAS_HOT_INDEXED_BRIDGES so post-recovery VACUUM still finds the
pages.  Data redirects are logged as (root, first_live) offset pairs followed by
each redirect's union bitmap (XLHP_HAS_REDIRECT_DATA); replay writes each blob
directly from its logged union and never walks the on-page chain, so it does not
depend on the adjacent tombstones (which the same record's NOW_UNUSED items may
reclaim) or on chain layout.  Replay is idempotent: re-applying the prune record
writes the same bridge and redirect bytes at the same LPs.
054_hot_indexed_bridge_recovery.pl exercises this under
wal_consistency_checking = all (with no pre-crash checkpoint, so the
tombstone/bridge/redirect redo paths actually run and are byte-compared to their
FPIs).


Novel line-pointer states and their invariants
-----------------------------------------------

HOT-indexed produces two line-pointer states classic HOT never does; both are
exercised by 054 under wal_consistency_checking.

  1. LP_DEAD on a heap-only tuple (aborted orphan).  An aborted HOT-indexed
     update leaves a dead heap-only tuple whose fresh btree leaf survives
     ROLLBACK until ambulkdelete sweeps it.  If no live chain root is reachable
     on the page to forward a bridge to, the LP is marked LP_DEAD (not
     LP_UNUSED): LP_DEAD pins the slot against reuse and enrols the TID in the
     dead-items array so ambulkdelete removes the stale leaf and a later VACUUM
     reclaims the LP.  heap_page_prune_execute's LP_DEAD assertion permits a
     heap-only tuple only when HEAP_INDEXED_UPDATED is set with natts > 0.

  2. A bridge forwarding to a non-live LP.  In the fully-dead-chain branch a
     bridge forwards to the chain root that the same prune is marking
     LP_DEAD/LP_UNUSED.  A reader following the bridge lands on the non-normal
     LP, where heap_hot_search_buffer terminates the walk -- correct for a dead
     chain.  amcheck therefore validates only that a bridge's forward offset is
     on the page and in range, NOT that it is currently LP_NORMAL.

  3. LP_REDIRECT with storage (a HOT-indexed data redirect).  Classic HOT
     redirects have lp_len == 0; a data redirect sets lp_len > 0 and stores a
     HotIndexedRedirectData body (HotIndexedRedirectIsData() is the
     discriminator).  Chain walkers read its target via
     HotIndexedRedirectGetTarget() instead of ItemIdGetRedirect().  When such a
     chain becomes fully dead the redirect is marked LP_DEAD like any redirect
     (its body dropped with the line pointer); heap_page_prune_execute's LP_DEAD
     assertion permits a redirect-with-storage for this case.


Tombstone reclamation
---------------------

  - Adjacent tombstone: prune_handle_tombstones keeps it while its target is a
    live HOT-indexed tuple OR a bridge (the per-hop bitmap is needed for the
    read-side overlap test as long as a stale leaf can reach the chain through
    that offset), and reclaims it to LP_UNUSED once the target becomes LP_UNUSED
    or LP_DEAD.  Consequently a tombstone whose tuple was just rewritten as a
    bridge survives one extra cycle: it is reclaimed by the VACUUM that reclaims
    the bridge, after ambulkdelete has swept the stale leaves.  Exception: at a
    single-hop collapse the live successor's tombstone is reclaimed immediately
    -- its bitmap is folded into the data redirect's union and a single-hop
    chain has no mid-chain leaf that would still consult it.
  - Bridge tombstone: reclaimed by lazy_vacuum_heap_page after ambulkdelete has
    swept the stale leaves that pointed at it.  When the last bridge on a page is
    reclaimed, PD_HAS_HOT_INDEXED_BRIDGES is cleared.

Per-hop tombstones are never coalesced.  Each hop's modified-attrs bitmap stays
in its own tombstone for the life of that hop; merging the mid-chain tombstones
into one bitmap would over-approximate the change set for a leaf pointing into
the middle of the chain and could drop a still-current row.  The data redirect's
union bitmap is not a counterexample: it is read only by a leaf pointing at the
chain root, which legitimately crosses every hop to first_live, so the union is
exactly that leaf's change set.  Mid-chain leaves never read the union; they
traverse the per-hop bridges and tombstones.


Prune lifecycle (state machine)
-------------------------------

Every line pointer in a HOT-indexed chain moves through a fixed set of states.
"stale leaf reachable" means a btree leaf may still point at this offset; only
ambulkdelete (the VACUUM index pass) clears that.

  LP_NORMAL live vN INDEXED_UPDATED{x}        -- a HOT-indexed row version
      |  version dies (HOT-updated again, deleted, or aborted)
      v
  dead member; prune classifies it (heap_prune_item_preserves_hot_indexed):
      |                                        |
      | no stale leaf possible                 | stale leaf may point here
      | (classic-HOT member, natts==0,         | (HEAP_INDEXED_UPDATED, natts>0)
      |  or XMIN_INVALID)                       |
      v                                        v
  LP_UNUSED  (reclaimed in place)          LP_NORMAL bridge (natts==0,
                                           t_ctid -> next survivor)
                                               |  ambulkdelete sweeps the
                                               |  stale leaves at this offset
                                               v
                                           dead_items -> lazy_vacuum_heap_page
                                               -> LP_UNUSED

Root collapse rules:

  - Multi-hop dead prefix:  LP[root] -> data redirect to first_live carrying
    the union of the skipped hops' bitmaps; each surviving mid-chain member
    keeps its own per-hop tombstone plus a bridge to the next survivor.  keep
    every per-hop bitmap.
  - Single-hop collapse:  the lone successor's tombstone is folded into the
    redirect union and dropped (no mid-chain leaf can consult it).
  - Purely classic prefix (no indexed hop):  LP[root] -> plain LP_REDIRECT to
    first_live (no bitmap), exactly as classic HOT.
  - Re-collapse:  a later prune of an already-collapsed chain re-points the
    existing data redirect at the new first_live and extends its union, so the
    redirect never dangles past a reclaimed member.  (Regression coverage for
    this and the full-VACUUM reclaim path is in hot_indexed_updates.sql.)

No-index relations:

  Bridge enrollment and reclamation are gated on nindexes > 0 -- with no index
  there are no stale leaves to sweep, and driving the indexed lazy_vacuum path
  would trip its nindexes > 0 assertion.  A relation never produces new
  HOT-indexed artifacts once it has no index (HeapUpdateHotAllowable sees an
  empty modified-indexed set and stays classic HOT).  If every index is dropped
  while pages still carry bridges/data redirects/tombstones, those artifacts
  persist and are followed transparently by reads; VACUUM neither crashes nor
  reclaims them.  Re-creating an index and vacuuming returns the chains to the
  normal reclamation path above.


All-visible vs tombstones
-------------------------

A page that holds any tombstone must never be marked PD_ALL_VISIBLE: the
all-visible seqscan fast path skips per-tuple visibility checks and would surface
a tombstone's payload bytes as a phantom row.  The prune/vacuum paths refuse to
set all-visible while a tombstone is present, and verify_heapam reports a
tombstone on an all-visible page as corruption.  A data redirect or bridge also
forces the page not-all-visible (an index-only scan must heap-fetch through the
chain so the read-side overlap test can filter stale leaves); the recorders set
prstate->set_all_visible = false.

The two reasons are distinct.  A tombstone (including the bridge variant) is an
LP_NORMAL carrier, so its hazard is the seqscan all-visible fast path, not
index-only scans.  A data redirect or bridge instead blocks index-only scans
specifically: a stale leaf reaching such a page must heap-fetch so the overlap
test can run.  Either way the suppression is precisely scoped and transient -- a
page is held not-all-visible only while it carries an artifact, and the same
VACUUM that sweeps its stale leaves and reclaims the artifact also sets the page
all-visible.  So a page is all-visible iff index-only scans through it are
provably safe.

Alternatives considered.  Teaching the seqscan fast path to skip tombstone line
pointers would let tombstone-only pages be all-visible, but it adds a branch to
the seqscan hot loop for every relation and does nothing for the index-only-scan
hazard, which is the harder one.  A third visibility-map state ("all-visible but
index-only scans must still recheck") would let artifact-bearing pages skip the
seqscan visibility checks while still forcing the IOS heap-fetch, but it is a
visibility-map format change touching every VM consumer and pg_upgrade, for a
benefit that lasts only until the next VACUUM.  Suppression is the minimal
correct policy.


CREATE INDEX / REINDEX
----------------------

A freshly built index is never stale: the build scans live tuples and inserts
entries with their current values.  For a HOT-indexed live tuple (one carrying
HEAP_INDEXED_UPDATED) the build points the new entry at the tuple's OWN TID, not
the chain root (heapam_index_build_range_scan / validate_scan), so the entry has
no crossed hop after it and the overlap test keeps it -- pointing such an entry
at the root would make the dead-prefix hops overlap and wrongly drop the row.
Tombstones are invisible to the build (HEAP_XMIN_INVALID), so they are skipped.
Build/REINDEX/CONCURRENTLY over tombstone-bearing pages is covered by the
hot_indexed_updates regression test.


Statistics and monitoring
-------------------------

  - pg_stat_all_tables.n_tup_hot_indexed_upd counts updates that took the
    HOT-indexed path (also counted in n_tup_hot_upd).
  - pg_stat_all_indexes.n_tup_hot_indexed_upd_{skipped,matched} count, per
    index, how many HOT-indexed updates skipped versus inserted into it.
  - pg_relation_hot_indexed_stats(regclass) returns
    (n_tombstones, n_chains, avg_chain_len, max_chain_len) under AccessShareLock.


Eligibility (no user-tunable knob)
----------------------------------

There is no user-facing setting for HOT-indexed.  An UPDATE takes the
HOT-indexed path when HeapUpdateHotAllowable() finds it eligible -- a
non-summarizing indexed attribute changed, the relation is neither a system
catalog nor has an exclusion constraint, and (on a logical-replication
subscriber) the apply-path gating allows it -- and the per-relation
chain-length cap below is not exceeded.  Whether the path is worthwhile is a
property of the relation, not something an operator can usefully tune, so it is
decided heuristically rather than by a GUC.

Chain-length cap.  RelationGetHotIndexedChainMax derives a per-relation maximum
chain length from fillfactor and estimated tuple size against the worst-case
tombstone size; heap_update downgrades to non-HOT when the cap is reached, so a
hot row cannot grow an unbounded same-page chain.


Logical replication apply
--------------------------

Logical decoding strips the tombstone trailer from the UPDATE record
(DecodeUpdate) so subscribers receive an ordinary UPDATE.  Whether the apply
worker itself uses the HOT-indexed path is gated per subscription by
pg_subscription.subhotindexedonapply (GetHotIndexedApplyMode):

    off          force non-HOT when the subscriber carries any indexed
                 attribute beyond its primary key
    subset_only  allow HOT-indexed when the subscriber's indexed-attr set is a
                 subset of its PK attrs (default)
    always       unconditional


System catalogs
---------------

System catalogs keep classic HOT (eligibility steps 1-2), but do NOT take the
HOT-indexed path (step 3): a catalog update that changes a non-summarizing
indexed attribute falls back to a non-HOT update, exactly as before this
feature.  Catalogs are reached through many access paths -- systable index
scans, SnapshotDirty unique checks, and seqscans in orderings the chain-walk
dedup does not cover -- and not all of them have been proven safe against stale
chain entries, so enabling HOT-indexed on catalogs is deliberately out of scope.
Because catalogs never take the HOT-indexed path, a catalog chain never carries
a tombstone and a systable scan never sees xs_hot_indexed_stale; the read path
therefore needs no catalog-specific handling.


On-disk compatibility and line-pointer consumers
------------------------------------------------

HOT-indexed reuses existing on-disk slots rather than adding catalog state: a
tombstone is an LP_NORMAL item with natts == 0, a bridge is the same with a
valid same-page t_ctid, and a data redirect is an LP_REDIRECT whose lp_len > 0
(a plain HOT redirect has lp_len == 0).  Two pitfalls for code that walks line
pointers follow from this:

  - ItemIdHasStorage() is true for a data redirect, yet it is not a heap tuple.
    Code that reads a stored item as a HeapTupleHeader must gate on
    ItemIdIsNormal(), not ItemIdHasStorage() (see index_delete_check_htid in
    heapam.c and heap_page_items in contrib/pageinspect, both of which do).
  - ItemIdGetRedirect() returns lp_off, which for a data redirect is the blob's
    byte offset, not a target offset.  Same-page chain walks must use
    HotIndexedRedirectGetTarget(), which resolves both redirect kinds.

Audited consumers: the prune/vacuum chain walks, heap_get_root_tuples (CLUSTER,
VACUUM FULL, index build), heap_index_delete_tuples, the seqscan and index-scan
paths, contrib/pageinspect, contrib/pgstattuple, contrib/pg_surgery, and
contrib/amcheck all resolve or skip the new states.  PageRepairFragmentation
needs no change: a data redirect's lp_off/lp_len describe its blob like any
stored item, so it relocates correctly.  heap_mask masks only LP_NORMAL hint
bits, so WAL consistency checking compares a data redirect's deterministic blob
as-is.

pg_upgrade.  A cluster upgraded from stock PostgreSQL holds no HOT-indexed
artifacts, so an upgrade into this server is unconditionally safe; artifacts
appear only from updates run afterwards.  pg_upgrade copies heap files verbatim,
so a cluster that already holds artifacts keeps them across an upgrade and the
target server must understand this format.  Every artifact is transient (the
next VACUUM of a page reclaims it), so draining them before an upgrade is
optional, not required.

Forward compatibility.  These line-pointer states are readable only by a server
carrying this feature; a build without it would misread a data redirect's blob.
The encoding has no format-version byte -- it is identified structurally
(lp_flags with lp_len, natts, t_ctid) -- so any future on-disk change must keep
those discriminators or add one.  verify_heapam validates all three states,
including the data redirect's blob bounds and length/bitmap-size agreement, so a
mismatch is detected rather than silently misinterpreted.


Limitations and restrictions
----------------------------

  - System catalogs: classic HOT only; HOT-indexed disabled (see above).
  - Exclusion constraints (including temporal PRIMARY KEY ... WITHOUT OVERLAPS):
    relation exempted; check_exclusion_or_unique_constraint relies on "one live
    tuple per (key, TID)", which stale chain entries break.

All non-summarizing index kinds participate, including non-btree access methods
and btree indexes with expression keys: the read-side overlap test uses only the
index's referenced heap attributes, so it needs neither a key comparison nor an
index tuple.


Glossary
--------

Fresh leaf entry
    A btree leaf inserted by a HOT-indexed update.  Its TID points directly at
    the heap-only tuple current at insert time.  A reader reaching the live
    tuple through a fresh entry computes an empty overlap (no covered attribute
    changed after the entry's target) and keeps the row.

Stale leaf entry
    A pre-update leaf whose key is an old value and whose TID still reaches the
    chain through the root (or an earlier member).  A chain walk through it
    crosses a HOT-indexed hop that changed a covered attribute; the overlap is
    non-empty and the leaf is discarded.

HEAP_INDEXED_UPDATED
    An infomask2 bit (0x0800).  Set on heap tuples and chain members produced by
    a HOT-indexed update, and on tombstones.  On a tuple it tells readers the
    next hop crossed a HOT-indexed write; on an item with natts == 0 it marks the
    item as a tombstone rather than a live tuple.

Modified-attrs bitmap
    A bitmap in an adjacent tombstone's body listing the indexed attributes that
    changed in that update hop.  The read-side overlap test ORs the bitmaps of
    the hops crossed after a leaf's target and intersects them with the arriving
    index's attribute set; the same bitmaps feed pg_relation_hot_indexed_stats.

hot-indexed hop
    A chain step whose source tuple has HEAP_INDEXED_UPDATED set.  Crossing at
    least one such hop after a leaf's target can set
    IndexScanDesc.xs_hot_indexed_stale on the returned tuple, depending on
    whether the hop's bitmap overlaps the index's attributes.
